sir david attenborough gazes down on a  street in dublin's portobello the 2019  mural was painted to celebrate his 93rd  birthday but it's now at the center of a  legal battle it was painted by the art  collective subset who also created a  mural of uk rapper stormzy  but along with two other murals like  horseboy the attenborough work was  created without permission from dublin  city council who decided to prosecute  one of subset's co-founders they remain  anonymous in public  told us of the toll it's taken  we always say it's so serious it's a  joke  so it's very difficult very stressful  the impact it has on the crew our  resources our time our energy  and  yeah it's difficult  this is not vandalism we paint with the  permission of property owners but in  ireland you also need planning  permission to create a mural  subset says the law is outdated and  needs to be overhauled it wants  regulation like that in sydney where  murals can be created with just the  building owner's consent as long as it's  not offensive you know our ultimate  motivation is a change in legislation  which will  as far as we're concerned be a catalyst  for  you know  more creative exploits  the city council told us it can't  comment on the subset case because it's  still before the courts but broadly  speaking the council says it is  supportive of public art installations  in the right appropriate locations the  council says it's approved no fewer than  35 large-scale works in the past year  alone  murals and other street art have  proliferated in ireland in recent years  owen conlon guides walking tours of  dublin's street art and says it helps  reduce anti-social behavior  this public art gallery brings people  down this lane constantly before there  would have been not many people visiting  this laneway now there is always  hundreds of people in that traffic just  reduces the issues that would have been  found here the case against subset has  now been adjourned until september you  know as far as we're concerned now it's  just bite down on the gum shield and  finish off the fight  and as far as you're concerned that only  ends with a change in the law yeah 100  it's a struggle between freedom of  expression and the regulation of civic  space played out against a very public  backdrop  stephen murphy sky news dublin
 

